<main id="main">
  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="hero-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-check"/></svg></span>
      <p class="kicker">Guide</p>
      <h1>Data licensing checklist</h1>
      <p class="lead">What to gather before a first conversation. None of it requires a project: it is the list a buyer will work through anyway.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to have ready</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Your systems</strong>The tools that hold the work: email, chat, file storage, support, CRM, project boards.</li>
        <li><strong>Years covered</strong>How far back each one goes, and whether older records are still accessible.</li>
        <li><strong>Who appears</strong>Customers, staff, suppliers or third parties named or identifiable in the records.</li>
        <li><strong>Contracts</strong>The customer agreements and tool terms that mention confidentiality, reuse or sharing.</li>
        <li><strong>What to exclude</strong>Anything privileged, regulated or simply not worth including.</li>
        <li><strong>Who approves</strong>The person who can sign off on scope and terms for your company.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Why each item matters</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap reveal">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>What to gather</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Systems and years</td><td>Whether there is enough history and variety to be useful to an AI team.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>People in the records</td><td>How much personal information has to be removed or transformed before delivery.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer agreements</td><td>Whether licensing is permitted at all, and whether notice or consent is needed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tool terms</td><td>Whether the platform allows your company to reuse and licence that content.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Exclusions</td><td>The scope written into the agreement, and what is never shared.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Questions you will be asked</h2>
      <ul class="examples reveal">
        <li>Which tools hold the records?</li>
        <li>How many years does each cover?</li>
        <li>Are customers identifiable?</li>
        <li>Do your contracts restrict sharing?</li>
        <li>Has anything been deleted or archived?</li>
        <li>Who signs off internally?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small-note">You do not need perfect answers. The point of the first call is to find out what is clear and what needs checking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Common questions</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal">
        <details><summary>Do I have to prepare exports first?</summary><p>No. The first conversation is about what exists and where it sits. Exports only matter once the rights and scope are clear.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if records are split across tools?</summary><p>That is normal, and usually a good sign. Records spread across several systems tend to carry more context than one archive.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How long does the review take?</summary><p>The data buyer generally expects about three months from the first conversation to payment, depending on scope, rights review and export.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

